WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.860
So as I mentioned, this is a plugin, therefore it does need to be enabled.

2
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.060
This is not enabled by default, and therefore you should go to your edit plugins section

3
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.460
and make sure that you've turned smart objects on if you want to replicate any of this functionality.

4
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.460
This will already be enabled in the test project.

5
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.059
There's an example scene which we'll go to in a little bit.

6
00:00:19.059 --> 00:00:21.059
So it's already enabled there if you're using that,

7
00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:26.670
but if you're trying to implement this on your own, just be aware it's a distinct plugin that you would need to enable.

8
00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.269
So what even are smart objects?

9
00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:36.869
Essentially, smart objects are there to solve a problem or an implementation that you may have,

10
00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:40.770
if you've worked in games that utilize AI,

11
00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:46.270
it's quite likely that you've implemented something like this yourself already,

12
00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:53.670
and you see a lot of interactions like it in any kind of game that might have behaviors

13
00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.070
that are numerous that can be performed by actors,

14
00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:03.670
and you wouldn't necessarily want the actor to know all of the things they can possibly do when they do it.

15
00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:07.870
So take for example, I don't know for a fact that they do this by the way,

16
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:11.069
but take for an example an NPC in Assassin's Creed, right?

17
00:01:11.069 --> 00:01:14.870
They walk around and they do things and occasionally they'll go up and sit down on the bench

18
00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.069
and they can probably do things like leaning against a wall,

19
00:01:17.069 --> 00:01:19.969
they can do things like, I don't know, shopping at a cart.

20
00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:23.569
I don't exactly know all the things that AIs do in Assassin's Creed,

21
00:01:23.569 --> 00:01:26.870
but I know that sitting on the bench is a thing they do.

22
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:34.069
So it might not make sense for us to code all of that logic into the AI itself,

23
00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:37.370
that it knows it can do these thousand different actions.

24
00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:43.969
It kind of makes more sense that we bake that functionality into the object that is being interacted with,

25
00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:45.569
and then anything can interact with it.

26
00:01:45.569 --> 00:01:49.269
We don't need to code this for the 20 different kinds of AIs that can use these things.

27
00:01:49.269 --> 00:01:55.560
All we do is kind of tie the things that are actually tightly related together.

28
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.359
So we have the object that is interacted with,

29
00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:01.760
that is the thing that is responsible for how do you interact with me,

30
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.959
here are the steps that you go through.

31
00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:08.560
And all the AI needs to know is that these things exist in the world,

32
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.360
I can perform these interactions,

33
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:15.659
there's maybe some additional querying they can do to be like, which action should I choose?

34
00:02:15.659 --> 00:02:21.960
But once they get to that action, it is then the action that tells them how to engage with it.

35
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.259
And at the same time, this idea that, you know,

36
00:02:26.259 --> 00:02:30.159
we don't want two NPCs trying to perform the same action at the same time,

37
00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:33.860
because, you know, if it's something like leaning against a wall or sitting on a bench,

38
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.060
there's only a certain amount of space on that bench,

39
00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.879
the two NPCs can't lean against the same wall and it would look silly if they did.

40
00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:44.180
So it's quite likely that if you again,

41
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.379
if you've worked with AI, you've already implemented a system a little bit like this.

42
00:02:48.379 --> 00:02:51.979
I certainly have done myself in previous games,

43
00:02:51.979 --> 00:02:53.680
smart objects didn't exist at that time,

44
00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.580
but I totally would have used them if they had.

45
00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.379
But essentially smart objects is a plugin that implements this in a kind of nice editor friendly way

46
00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:03.080
with a bit of optimization on top.

47
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:09.979
So these smart objects are essentially objects that AIs and players can interact with.

48
00:03:09.979 --> 00:03:11.479
And they, like I said,

49
00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:18.080
the object itself contains all of the information required to actually handle the interaction.

50
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.180
All of these interactions are grouped together into a global database

51
00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.830
that is then accessible and queryable by anyone who's interested,

52
00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:31.729
and also that these objects utilize partitioning logic

53
00:03:31.729 --> 00:03:34.330
so they can be queried at runtime in a more kind of performant way.

54
00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:39.629
So you're not, you can kind of ignore interactions that are two kilometers away

55
00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:42.330
and focus just on the, just for performance reasons

56
00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.830
and also just for kind of logic reasons,

57
00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:47.930
you can be focused a little bit more on the things that are closer to you,

58
00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.229
as well as this idea of reservations so that, you know,

59
00:03:51.229 --> 00:03:54.930
we don't have a situation where two actors are traversing, you know,

60
00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.930
over 20 meters to both get to the same melon to pick up

61
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.330
and then only at that point do they realize,

62
00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:06.280
oh, this other guy was going to pick up the melon and therefore I can't.

63
00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.280
So the five types that we should be aware of when we're,

64
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.879
what are the kind of five fundamental types of be aware of with a smart object system

65
00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:15.080
is the subsystem, smart object collections,

66
00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.379
the component, definitions, and then behavior definitions.

67
00:04:18.379 --> 00:04:20.980
We're going to talk a little bit more about each of those.

68
00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.180
The smart object subsystem,

69
00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.079
if you're already familiar with unreal subsystems,

70
00:04:25.079 --> 00:04:27.879
this is, you can kind of imagine a little bit what this is.

71
00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:31.579
This is essentially the kind of primary point of interaction.

72
00:04:31.579 --> 00:04:35.480
It's a single entry point.

73
00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:38.379
It's only instantiated on servers and multiplayer games

74
00:04:38.379 --> 00:04:40.480
as we've already spoken about with a lot of AI stuff.

75
00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.279
Really AI is handled by the server

76
00:04:42.279 --> 00:04:44.779
because it is the server that has authoritative state

77
00:04:44.779 --> 00:04:47.079
that should be responsible for decision making.

78
00:04:47.079 --> 00:04:52.680
Again, the pawns, the things that the other clients would see

79
00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.959
are really just there to parrot out the behavior that the server is deciding.

80
00:04:57.959 --> 00:04:59.860
And it's the smart object subsystem

81
00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.160
that really couples all of this stuff together.

82
00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.060
The smart object subsystem is our entry point

83
00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.560
full of our queries and logic

84
00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.560
for anyone outside of the actual system itself.

85
00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.160
They would query in through the subsystem.

86
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.160
So the subsystem is the kind of like thing

87
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:20.459
that exposes all of this functionality to other agents, actors, players.

88
00:05:20.459 --> 00:05:22.860
It's also the subsystem that's responsible

89
00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.560
for keeping track of all of our smart objects

90
00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.160
that are actually in our level.

91
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.459
And yeah, it's automatically created in level

92
00:05:30.459 --> 00:05:32.660
and only one of these would exist at any given time.

93
00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:36.459
So that's a kind of normal thing for a lot of subsystems.

94
00:05:36.459 --> 00:05:38.959
The vast, if you're familiar with unreal subsystems,

95
00:05:38.959 --> 00:05:40.060
you would kind of know these things

96
00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:41.660
are kind of automatically created for us,

97
00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:43.759
they're automatically cleaned up for us.

98
00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:47.860
And for the majority of types, there will only ever be one.

99
00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:52.500
The only one that's contrary to that I can think of

100
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.589
is a local player subsystem, which this isn't.

101
00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.889
Yeah, so then we've got the smart object collection.

102
00:05:58.889 --> 00:06:02.790
This is, again, only one of these exists per level.

103
00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.889
And this is essentially just a list

104
00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:08.089
of all the smart object components associated with the level.

105
00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:13.290
And it's used to create the instance of those smart objects at runtime.

106
00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.389
So, you know, the components linked to the collection,

107
00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:17.189
the collection makes the appropriate smart objects

108
00:06:17.189 --> 00:06:19.089
for those components.

109
00:06:19.089 --> 00:06:23.990
And it's essentially just a place to gather all these things together.

110
00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.689
The smart object component is a component

111
00:06:25.689 --> 00:06:27.290
that can be added to any actor.

112
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.790
And it's the component itself that stores the information

113
00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.389
about what kind of smart object is this.

114
00:06:32.389 --> 00:06:34.790
And then this component would then get registered

115
00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:35.990
with the smart object collection,

116
00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.689
which will create the appropriate smart objects for it.

117
00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:40.889
The actor containing the smart object component

118
00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:43.589
can be loaded and unloaded at runtime.

119
00:06:43.589 --> 00:06:45.790
You know, they're kind of in a large world.

120
00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:47.490
This might happen through world partitioning

121
00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.089
or through streaming those levels in and out.

122
00:06:49.089 --> 00:06:49.990
And this isn't a problem.

123
00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:53.089
The system accounts for these things.

124
00:06:53.089 --> 00:06:57.389
And, yeah, if the actor containing the smart object component

125
00:06:57.389 --> 00:06:59.790
is spawned at runtime,

126
00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:03.290
then it's not going to remain in memory once it's unloaded.

127
00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:07.790
So this is more about those kind of permanent fixtures within the level.

128
00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:12.889
If it's spawned in and out at runtime,

129
00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:15.089
then that's not going to have that same level of persistence

130
00:07:15.089 --> 00:07:16.790
within the system because, you know,

131
00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:19.290
that's the kind of nature of these runtime things.

132
00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.290
That's then your responsibility to know

133
00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:23.189
how long that's meant to stay around for.

134
00:07:23.189 --> 00:07:25.790
The kind of overall system is really meant for things

135
00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:27.689
that are more like fixtures in the world.

136
00:07:27.689 --> 00:07:30.189
And the more temporary things are things

137
00:07:30.189 --> 00:07:33.240
that you would kind of have to manage independently.

138
00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.639
So smart object definition is actually the kind of,

139
00:07:36.639 --> 00:07:39.939
the key object that groups together this behavior.

140
00:07:39.939 --> 00:07:44.639
This is the kind of, well, definition,

141
00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:46.740
don't use the word to define the word,

142
00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.339
but it's, yeah, the immutable data that defines this thing.

143
00:07:49.339 --> 00:07:52.339
So this isn't manipulated at runtime.

144
00:07:52.339 --> 00:07:56.139
It simply exposes what does this smart object look like

145
00:07:56.139 --> 00:07:59.939
at a kind of editor level, at a kind of immutable way.

146
00:07:59.939 --> 00:08:03.740
That might be these actions as part of this object definition

147
00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:05.939
is these actions are available.

148
00:08:05.939 --> 00:08:08.639
They're at these locations, at these rotations.

149
00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:12.740
They have particular tags and markup and all of these,

150
00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:16.240
a single definition can contain multiple slots

151
00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:19.639
and it's the slots that agents would choose

152
00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:20.439
to interact with.

153
00:08:20.439 --> 00:08:23.040
So again, going back to like a bench

154
00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.740
that might be a smart object

155
00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.139
and then that bench might have multiple slots

156
00:08:28.139 --> 00:08:30.439
for every place that you could sit on that bench.

157
00:08:30.439 --> 00:08:33.840
And this is kind of like a static representation

158
00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.940
of what this bench interaction would look like.

159
00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:38.840
And then you can drop multiple of those around the world

160
00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.039
through a smart object component,

161
00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:42.539
through an actor utilizing that smart object component

162
00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:45.539
and to then have several benches throughout your level.

163
00:08:45.539 --> 00:08:47.039
But the idea of a bench,

164
00:08:47.039 --> 00:08:50.240
the fact that there are three, four, whatever seats

165
00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.440
at these separations with these rotations

166
00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.639
and these tags on them,

167
00:08:54.639 --> 00:08:56.539
none of that is stuff that is necessarily

168
00:08:56.539 --> 00:08:59.139
going to change at runtime.

169
00:09:00.159 --> 00:09:01.659
So the smart object definition

170
00:09:01.659 --> 00:09:04.860
is this kind of like static editor level definition

171
00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:08.059
of how this thing can be interacted with.

172
00:09:08.059 --> 00:09:10.360
And then the behavior definition

173
00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.460
is the thing that occurs essentially each slot.

174
00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:16.059
This is a little bit like our task.

175
00:09:16.059 --> 00:09:18.759
So this is the thing that actually gets performed.

176
00:09:18.759 --> 00:09:20.960
The smart object definition groups these things together,

177
00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:21.960
defines them by slots

178
00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.759
and these slots can have behaviors linked to them

179
00:09:24.759 --> 00:09:27.059
and it's behaviors themselves

180
00:09:27.059 --> 00:09:29.759
that are the things that the agents would do.

181
00:09:29.759 --> 00:09:33.960
So the AI agent, player agent or whatever,

182
00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.259
this is the kind of functionality that they will perform

183
00:09:36.259 --> 00:09:37.960
whether that's playing animations,

184
00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:39.360
whether it's playing sounds,

185
00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.360
whether that's moving to particular locations,

186
00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:42.960
whatever that needs to be,

187
00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:45.860
that this is the actual thing

188
00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.460
that would contain the logic

189
00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.759
for doing those interactions.

190
00:09:51.159 --> 00:09:53.059
There are a couple of pre-existing examples.

191
00:09:53.059 --> 00:09:54.759
So we've got a gameplay behavior.

192
00:09:54.759 --> 00:09:58.759
So this is for any kind of poem or actor to perform.

193
00:09:58.759 --> 00:10:02.259
Player behavior is specifically built for player characters

194
00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.759
and then mass entity,

195
00:10:03.759 --> 00:10:05.159
we'll talk about mass in a little bit,

196
00:10:05.159 --> 00:10:07.259
but this is a behavior that makes use of,

197
00:10:09.159 --> 00:10:12.659
it can basically a smart object interaction definition

198
00:10:12.659 --> 00:10:14.659
that can interact with smart entities

199
00:10:14.659 --> 00:10:17.360
rather than usual kind of gameplay behaviors.

200
00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:20.159
Again, we'll touch on mass entity in a little bit.

201
00:10:20.159 --> 00:10:22.460
So that's just kind of a brief summary

202
00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:23.659
of all of the types.

203
00:10:25.159 --> 00:10:27.960
I'll actually just quickly demonstrate this functionality.

204
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.960
So if you open up the project,

205
00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:30.860
you'll see that it's actually,

206
00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:34.159
we've got our own little example of that.

207
00:10:34.159 --> 00:10:36.659
This is essentially a re-implementation

208
00:10:36.659 --> 00:10:39.960
of the Unreal documentation.

209
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.659
So if you've already been through that,

210
00:10:42.659 --> 00:10:46.059
you'll see that a lot of this is relatively similar.

211
00:10:46.059 --> 00:10:48.559
So if you go to just Unreal's

212
00:10:48.559 --> 00:10:49.960
getting started with smart objects

213
00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:51.860
or smart object quick start,

214
00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:53.360
you'll end up inventing something

215
00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:54.659
a little bit similar to this.

216
00:10:54.659 --> 00:10:57.460
So you don't need to do both of those things,

217
00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:58.759
but this adds just a little bit

218
00:10:58.759 --> 00:10:59.860
of extra functionality on top

219
00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.559
that I thought was kind of pertinent.

220
00:11:01.559 --> 00:11:04.559
So one of the things that they don't really highlight

221
00:11:04.559 --> 00:11:07.159
is the kind of benefits and meanings

222
00:11:07.159 --> 00:11:08.460
of this reservation system.

223
00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:09.960
So just to kind of quickly talk through

224
00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.960
the various things that we've got here,

225
00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:17.360
we've got the actual smart object itself.

226
00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:19.360
So we've got this smart object definition.

227
00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:20.460
This, like we said,

228
00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.159
is the thing that represents

229
00:11:22.159 --> 00:11:24.659
the kind of static editor level data

230
00:11:24.659 --> 00:11:28.259
for what are the different available options

231
00:11:28.259 --> 00:11:30.460
when this smart objects exists in world,

232
00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:33.159
how can it be interacted with in what different places

233
00:11:33.159 --> 00:11:34.460
or what different rotations

234
00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:36.960
and what behaviors are linked to each of these things?

235
00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.360
So in this case, we just have a single slot

236
00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.360
and it's got this particular behavior that we've put on it

237
00:11:45.059 --> 00:11:47.759
and we've just dropped a couple of actors

238
00:11:47.759 --> 00:11:51.659
utilizing that component,

239
00:11:51.659 --> 00:11:54.259
utilizing that slot into the world.

240
00:11:54.259 --> 00:11:58.730
So that's just this actor here

241
00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:01.830
and as you can see, this is just called play montage

242
00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.330
because that's really what the behavior is doing

243
00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.029
and you can see we use the smart object component.

244
00:12:07.029 --> 00:12:08.429
In that smart object component,

245
00:12:08.429 --> 00:12:10.230
we've plugged in that smart object definition.

246
00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:12.330
So again, you can see these kind of relationships

247
00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:13.330
all linking up.

248
00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:16.429
The smart object definition implements the behaviors.

249
00:12:16.429 --> 00:12:20.830
The component says what definition should it be using

250
00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:23.629
and then the actor that uses that component,

251
00:12:23.629 --> 00:12:25.330
we've dragged several of those actors around

252
00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:27.929
to essentially drop several of these behaviors

253
00:12:27.929 --> 00:12:28.929
around our world.

254
00:12:30.559 --> 00:12:32.960
So the extra kind of, and then just on top of that,

255
00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:35.460
so again, we've got the definition,

256
00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.159
we've got the actual behaviors

257
00:12:37.159 --> 00:12:38.360
that those definitions are doing

258
00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:40.259
and then we've got a behavior tree

259
00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.360
that actually just utilizes some of this behavior.

260
00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:47.159
So you'll notice when you enable

261
00:12:47.159 --> 00:12:49.659
the smart object plugin,

262
00:12:49.659 --> 00:12:51.759
if you go to your blackboard,

263
00:12:51.759 --> 00:12:54.460
there's another kind of blackboard key that gets added,

264
00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:56.759
which is this smart object claim handle.

265
00:12:56.759 --> 00:12:58.659
This relates to this reservation system

266
00:12:58.659 --> 00:12:59.460
that we were talking about.

267
00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.159
It's basically the idea that an agent

268
00:13:01.159 --> 00:13:02.860
can decide ahead of time,

269
00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:06.460
I am going to seek out a smart object.

270
00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:09.059
So that's obviously using this smart object subsystem

271
00:13:09.059 --> 00:13:11.460
and accessing these collections that we've talked about.

272
00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.659
I'm gonna find a smart object that I want to use

273
00:13:13.659 --> 00:13:15.460
if there is one available.

274
00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:17.059
And then the point that I've decided

275
00:13:17.059 --> 00:13:17.860
I'm going to use it,

276
00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:20.460
I'm going to reserve it so no one else can take it.

277
00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:21.659
Then I'm gonna move to it

278
00:13:21.659 --> 00:13:25.059
and then do what other interaction it defines.

279
00:13:25.159 --> 00:13:28.259
So most of that is kind of done

280
00:13:28.259 --> 00:13:31.559
through this getting started or the quick start,

281
00:13:31.559 --> 00:13:33.259
but the kind of thing that it doesn't particularly

282
00:13:33.259 --> 00:13:35.659
emphasize is the meaning of this reservation.

283
00:13:35.659 --> 00:13:37.200
So I thought I would just kind of demonstrate

284
00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.200
that a little bit.

285
00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.759
What I've done here is if we just look

286
00:13:39.759 --> 00:13:41.860
at each of these actors,

287
00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:44.820
I've just added this kind of cube above each of them.

288
00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:51.500
And you'll see that when a smart object actually changes,

289
00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:53.460
so on our smart object component,

290
00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:55.860
we've just hooked up to an event to basically say that,

291
00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.659
hey, when our smart object has been claimed

292
00:13:58.659 --> 00:14:02.259
or when it's been released, set the material on this cube.

293
00:14:02.259 --> 00:14:04.059
So essentially this cube will denote to us

294
00:14:04.059 --> 00:14:06.259
when someone has kind of claimed this smart object

295
00:14:06.259 --> 00:14:08.259
when they said, hey, I'm the one who's gonna use this.

296
00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:10.559
And when it's no longer being used,

297
00:14:10.559 --> 00:14:12.759
it's kind of the material has changed back.

298
00:14:12.759 --> 00:14:15.460
And the whole idea here is that we have three objects

299
00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:16.559
that can be interacted with

300
00:14:16.559 --> 00:14:19.289
before people who want to do the interacting.

301
00:14:19.289 --> 00:14:21.789
All of these use the same behavior tree.

302
00:14:21.789 --> 00:14:23.690
So they're trying to access the same data.

303
00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:28.690
And you can see here that it's most obvious

304
00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:29.889
right at the beginning,

305
00:14:29.889 --> 00:14:31.690
you'll see three of the agents

306
00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:33.789
will just instantly run to these objects.

307
00:14:33.789 --> 00:14:35.389
That's because the first thing they've done

308
00:14:35.389 --> 00:14:38.690
is looked for an object to utilize

309
00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:41.690
and they've reserved it and then they move over to it

310
00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:42.690
and then do the interaction.

311
00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.690
The interaction is just this jump.

312
00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.590
And then the fourth agent,

313
00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:47.990
because there's nothing available,

314
00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:48.889
they just don't do anything.

315
00:14:48.889 --> 00:14:51.490
They just sit still until whichever agent

316
00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.090
got to its thing first and finished the interaction

317
00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:55.789
instantly at that point it's released

318
00:14:55.789 --> 00:14:58.090
and that fourth agent will then move

319
00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:03.580
to that first available thing to actually interact with it.

320
00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.980
So we see instantly it flickers green

321
00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:06.879
and then go red again,

322
00:15:06.879 --> 00:15:09.179
because as soon as it's available,

323
00:15:09.179 --> 00:15:11.379
if there was an agent waiting,

324
00:15:11.379 --> 00:15:13.309
it will then get reserved.

325
00:15:13.309 --> 00:15:14.809
So this just kind of demonstrates

326
00:15:14.809 --> 00:15:16.710
that reservation functionality

327
00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:18.840
a little bit more comprehensively.

328
00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:19.840
So that's smart objects.

329
00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:21.840
Obviously with all of these plugins,

330
00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:23.539
there's a lot more detail we can go into.

331
00:15:23.539 --> 00:15:25.440
This is kind of more just to act as a little bit

332
00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.740
of an introduction to say what it's there to achieve

333
00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:30.639
and kind of roughly how you can interact with it,

334
00:15:30.639 --> 00:15:32.840
but absolutely kind of dig into that more

335
00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:34.720
in your own time.

336
00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:36.919
So next up is state trees.

337
00:15:36.919 --> 00:15:41.820
State trees is essentially a blend of behavior trees

338
00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:42.820
and state machines.

339
00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:45.720
So if you might be familiar with state machines already,

340
00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:46.820
we talked about them a little bit

341
00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:48.919
in the second session if you've seen that,

342
00:15:48.919 --> 00:15:50.519
because state machines are used quite a lot

343
00:15:50.519 --> 00:15:51.919
in animation blueprints.

344
00:15:51.919 --> 00:15:53.919
State machines, if you're not familiar with them,

345
00:15:53.919 --> 00:15:56.820
are essentially the idea that a particular state

346
00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:00.820
can be represented by a kind of node or data.

347
00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:04.220
And there are rules that govern how we can transition

348
00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:06.019
from one state to others.

349
00:16:06.019 --> 00:16:09.620
So giving animation as an example,

350
00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:11.419
you could be in your idle state

351
00:16:11.419 --> 00:16:14.919
and then from that idle state, hypothetically,

352
00:16:14.919 --> 00:16:17.320
we could transition into our jump state

353
00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:19.620
or our running state based on different rules.

354
00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.720
If we're jumping, then we transition to our jump state.

355
00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:25.220
If we're running, then we transition to our running state.

356
00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:27.820
If we're kind of moving and jumping,

357
00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:29.620
then certain rules will define

358
00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:30.919
that jumping is higher priority

359
00:16:30.919 --> 00:16:33.120
and therefore we still transition to our jumping state

360
00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.919
even though we are both moving and jumping.

361
00:16:35.919 --> 00:16:37.820
So obviously there is also this idea

362
00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:40.019
that it's not just one or the other.

363
00:16:40.019 --> 00:16:42.120
Sometimes you have a competition,

364
00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:43.919
you need to think about priorities

365
00:16:43.919 --> 00:16:45.419
or you need to think about extra rules

366
00:16:45.419 --> 00:16:47.320
to govern which of these two states

367
00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:49.019
should we transition to into

368
00:16:49.019 --> 00:16:51.879
even if hypothetically both of them are possible.

369
00:16:51.879 --> 00:16:54.779
So that's the idea of state machines.

370
00:16:54.779 --> 00:16:56.179
And obviously even within that,

371
00:16:56.179 --> 00:16:58.779
you have this idea of the idea

372
00:16:58.779 --> 00:17:00.480
that when you are in a state,

373
00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:02.480
you can transition possibly back

374
00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:06.279
or perhaps a state can only transition in one direction.

375
00:17:06.279 --> 00:17:08.180
These are typical concepts for state machines.

376
00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:10.480
It's not an unreal unique thing,

377
00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.079
but then behavior tree is also not an unreal unique thing,

378
00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:17.480
but state tree kind of brings both of these things together.

379
00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:20.579
It brings over the functionality of state machines.

380
00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:25.079
So our AI logic,

381
00:17:25.079 --> 00:17:27.680
if we think about our behavior tree,

382
00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:31.579
we can basically make it a little bit more like a web

383
00:17:31.579 --> 00:17:33.380
so that we can transition

384
00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:35.079
if we were to kind of look at a behavior tree

385
00:17:35.079 --> 00:17:37.279
is this very kind of top-down hierarchy

386
00:17:37.279 --> 00:17:41.579
where we can't go from a fine smart object to wait

387
00:17:41.579 --> 00:17:42.779
if we wanted to,

388
00:17:42.779 --> 00:17:45.579
we have to kind of obey the rules of the existing system.

389
00:17:45.579 --> 00:17:46.480
We have to structure our things

390
00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:48.579
so we can negotiate between these things.

391
00:17:48.579 --> 00:17:51.180
Whereas state tree gives us more of the ability

392
00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:52.779
to go from each of our states

393
00:17:52.779 --> 00:17:54.880
and transition to any other state

394
00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.279
based on conditions that we kind of specify.

395
00:17:57.279 --> 00:18:01.279
So it's less of this pure top-down flow.

396
00:18:01.279 --> 00:18:03.079
At the same time though,

397
00:18:03.079 --> 00:18:05.279
a state tree is also hierarchical.

398
00:18:05.279 --> 00:18:08.480
So what that means is it exists in a hierarchical tree,

399
00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:12.880
meaning you can technically be in multiple states at once.

400
00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:15.279
It's a little bit similar to our behavior tree,

401
00:18:15.279 --> 00:18:19.680
the way that when I'm in my fine smart objects task,

402
00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:23.480
I am technically within, in an abstract sense,

403
00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:25.079
I'm within this branch of the tree

404
00:18:25.079 --> 00:18:26.279
and therefore I'm in sequence

405
00:18:26.279 --> 00:18:27.779
and therefore I'm in this selector,

406
00:18:27.779 --> 00:18:29.380
but it's a little bit more explicit

407
00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:30.680
and it gives us the capability

408
00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:35.779
to actually build in behavior to that hierarchy.

409
00:18:35.779 --> 00:18:36.680
So yeah, like I said,

410
00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:39.079
the states are arranged in a tree structure.

411
00:18:39.079 --> 00:18:41.779
The selection usually starts at the root

412
00:18:41.779 --> 00:18:44.279
and the state trees require a schema to be selected

413
00:18:44.279 --> 00:18:46.779
which is essentially how do we typically expect

414
00:18:46.779 --> 00:18:47.880
this state tree to be used?

415
00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:51.180
Like what is our typical context for this to be used?

416
00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:54.880
That might be on, it's part of a state tree component

417
00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:56.880
so we expect it to be on an actor.

418
00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:58.680
It might be a camera directed state tree

419
00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.880
if you're using a state tree to govern, you know,

420
00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:04.980
cameras in your game or in your cinematics.

421
00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:07.980
Like with a few of these different plugins,

422
00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:09.480
this isn't purely for AI.

423
00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:11.680
You can use state trees for other things.

424
00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:12.880
I think in a lot of the examples,

425
00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.180
they use state trees for animations.

426
00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:19.079
Obviously the parallels with state machines

427
00:19:19.079 --> 00:19:20.880
from animation being very obvious.

428
00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:24.480
So you could use state trees to govern things like,

429
00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:27.019
well, anything that has state really.

430
00:19:27.019 --> 00:19:29.579
So if you had, I think the typical example they give

431
00:19:29.579 --> 00:19:32.880
is like a chest that is, once you open it,

432
00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:35.579
it plays animation to be in the open state.

433
00:19:35.579 --> 00:19:39.579
You can imagine various uses for these outside of AI,

434
00:19:39.579 --> 00:19:44.990
but AI is one of the uses for this.

435
00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:50.259
So yes, so within a state tree,

436
00:19:50.259 --> 00:19:51.859
this is what it actually looks like.

437
00:19:51.859 --> 00:19:54.000
Again, we have this,

438
00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:57.059
you can see this tree structure already emerging.

439
00:19:57.059 --> 00:19:58.859
So just to kind of talk about this a little bit,

440
00:19:58.859 --> 00:20:00.420
we've got the root here.

441
00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:02.299
This is a kind of first selected state.

442
00:20:02.299 --> 00:20:04.240
This is a little bit like the root of our behavior tree

443
00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.200
as our entry point.

444
00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:08.900
And then we have these kind of sub nodes.

445
00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:12.460
So each of these are reacting,

446
00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:15.500
well, the state here that is called reacting state

447
00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:17.500
is actually called a selector state.

448
00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:21.539
This refers to a state that has child states within it.

449
00:20:21.539 --> 00:20:24.700
So the kind of important thing to be aware of with this,

450
00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.259
and it can be particularly confusing

451
00:20:27.259 --> 00:20:30.200
if you're trying to debug your state tree a little bit

452
00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:34.039
and you kind of maybe disable some of those states,

453
00:20:34.039 --> 00:20:35.099
it can lead to behavior

454
00:20:35.099 --> 00:20:37.359
that might not necessarily expect unless you know this,

455
00:20:37.359 --> 00:20:40.400
is that as soon as you put a state

456
00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:41.900
within another state,

457
00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:44.599
that state becomes what's called a selector state.

458
00:20:44.599 --> 00:20:47.240
And that means that this state's reacting

459
00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:49.039
can never be selected directly.

460
00:20:49.039 --> 00:20:52.740
You can't really be in the reacting state itself.

461
00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:54.960
All you can ever do once you have children

462
00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:56.700
is be in one of the child states.

463
00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:58.640
And that inherently implies

464
00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:00.740
that you are both in the reacting state

465
00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:03.940
and the flee state or handle obstacle state

466
00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:05.799
or hit state in this situation.

467
00:21:07.109 --> 00:21:09.210
So once a state has children,

468
00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:12.930
you can never choose specifically to be in that state.

469
00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:14.809
You can only be in one of its children,

470
00:21:14.809 --> 00:21:16.369
but by being in one of its children,

471
00:21:16.369 --> 00:21:18.869
it inherently implies that you are also in that state,

472
00:21:18.869 --> 00:21:21.309
bringing that kind of, again, that hierarchical flow.

473
00:21:21.309 --> 00:21:22.630
So as soon as you're in the flee state,

474
00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:25.069
you are also inherently part of the root,

475
00:21:25.069 --> 00:21:26.430
part of the reacting state.

476
00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:28.869
If you're within the smart object state here,

477
00:21:28.869 --> 00:21:30.529
well, if you're in the reach state,

478
00:21:30.529 --> 00:21:32.470
you are inherently part of the smart objects,

479
00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:35.230
selector state, part of the idling selector state,

480
00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:36.390
and part of the root.

481
00:21:38.359 --> 00:21:40.599
And then, yeah, if we have,

482
00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.960
we have this idea of the kind of state enter condition,

483
00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.539
which essentially refers to a list of conditions

484
00:21:47.539 --> 00:21:51.339
that determine whether or not this state can be selected.

485
00:21:51.339 --> 00:21:55.359
So if our state here can't be chosen

486
00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.500
based on certain logic that we can specify,

487
00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:02.359
again, this whole idea that as with the state machines,

488
00:22:02.359 --> 00:22:04.400
we can only transition into this state

489
00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:06.920
based on certain conditions.

490
00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:08.720
And then we actually have the task,

491
00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:11.180
which is the things that this state performs.

492
00:22:12.359 --> 00:22:13.859
So this is actually the kind of,

493
00:22:13.859 --> 00:22:16.180
when we're in the state, what do we actually do?

494
00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:18.180
What does this state actually mean?

495
00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:19.059
And then last of all,

496
00:22:19.059 --> 00:22:20.819
we have this concept of transitions,

497
00:22:20.819 --> 00:22:23.980
which is our ability to move from one state to another

498
00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:26.180
based on certain rules.

499
00:22:26.180 --> 00:22:27.480
We'll actually come to that in a little bit,

500
00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.779
but that can be kind of this state ending

501
00:22:29.779 --> 00:22:31.619
when a certain task completes

502
00:22:31.619 --> 00:22:34.839
or based on certain queries that we might be making

503
00:22:34.839 --> 00:22:37.759
during the kind of running of that state.

504
00:22:37.759 --> 00:22:39.259
So the kind of concepts

505
00:22:39.259 --> 00:22:41.019
that are important to bear in mind,

506
00:22:41.019 --> 00:22:43.200
states themselves, I feel like I'm gonna say state

507
00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:46.220
like a hundred times whilst I'm talking about this,

508
00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:49.059
but yeah, it's the state that has the conditions

509
00:22:49.059 --> 00:22:51.619
for when this, it can be entered.

510
00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:56.059
It will contain evaluators, tasks, and transitions.

511
00:22:56.059 --> 00:22:58.279
So that is really what a state is.

512
00:22:58.279 --> 00:23:00.859
Evaluators, we'll talk about in a little bit.

513
00:23:00.859 --> 00:23:02.480
Tasks, we'll kind of talk about in a little bit,

514
00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:03.819
but all of these things really,

515
00:23:03.819 --> 00:23:05.180
we'll talk about in a little bit,

516
00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:07.559
but it's the state itself kind of groups

517
00:23:07.559 --> 00:23:08.380
all of these things together.

518
00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:09.680
That's what kind of what a state is.

519
00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:14.680
It's a grouping of evaluators, tasks, and transitions.

520
00:23:16.269 --> 00:23:18.509
Selecting a state will activate all of the states

521
00:23:18.509 --> 00:23:19.710
from the root to the leaf.

522
00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:20.650
So this is what we're talking about,

523
00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:22.609
this hierarchical nature.

524
00:23:22.609 --> 00:23:24.809
So if I'm in the flea state,

525
00:23:24.809 --> 00:23:27.809
then automatically the reacting and root state

526
00:23:27.809 --> 00:23:30.710
are all active because it's inherently implied

527
00:23:30.710 --> 00:23:32.250
I'm in all of these states.

528
00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:37.250
And that kind of means that any additional tasks

529
00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:39.809
or any additional functionality

530
00:23:39.809 --> 00:23:41.150
that's introduced with each of these states

531
00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:43.609
is inherently brought in.

532
00:23:43.609 --> 00:23:46.150
If we have any ticks going on in the reacting state,

533
00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:47.210
whilst I'm in the flea state,

534
00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:49.410
those reacting state ticks would still be running

535
00:23:50.049 --> 00:23:51.710
because I am always in the reacting state

536
00:23:51.710 --> 00:23:53.890
when I'm in the flea state.

537
00:23:54.849 --> 00:23:56.750
Data exposed by any state will be available

538
00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:58.250
to other states further down the tree.

539
00:23:58.250 --> 00:23:59.750
So if we have, again,

540
00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:02.250
we'll talk about evaluators and parameters in a little bit,

541
00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:04.109
but if we have evaluators of parameters

542
00:24:04.109 --> 00:24:05.849
as part of the reacting state,

543
00:24:05.849 --> 00:24:08.990
all of those will be available to any of the child states.

544
00:24:11.329 --> 00:24:13.769
Yeah, all tasks are executed for all active states.

545
00:24:13.769 --> 00:24:15.170
So I've kind of already mentioned that,

546
00:24:15.170 --> 00:24:17.390
that when we're in the flea state,

547
00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:22.089
any tasks that we have on the reacting selector state

548
00:24:22.089 --> 00:24:23.150
would also be running.

549
00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:28.049
And a state can contain multiple tasks

550
00:24:28.049 --> 00:24:30.589
that are all running concurrently

551
00:24:30.589 --> 00:24:32.029
for as long as the state remains active.

552
00:24:32.029 --> 00:24:33.990
So that's kind of what tasks really mean.

553
00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:36.390
It gives us this ability to,

554
00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:38.730
whereas our behavior tree can only really be

555
00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:40.849
in any one node at a time,

556
00:24:40.849 --> 00:24:43.230
clearly from what we've talked about so far,

557
00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.130
state trees are a lot more parallel.

558
00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:46.829
They can have multiple tasks,

559
00:24:46.829 --> 00:24:48.130
not just within a single state,

560
00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:50.130
but you might have tasks within the parent states

561
00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.390
that are also executing simultaneously.

562
00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:57.240
So it's a lot more of this kind of parallel nature.

563
00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:59.240
A task, I feel like we've kind of already talked

564
00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:00.339
about that a little bit now,

565
00:25:00.339 --> 00:25:01.579
so it's probably obvious,

566
00:25:01.579 --> 00:25:04.779
but tasks similar to how tasks work in behavior tree,

567
00:25:04.779 --> 00:25:06.019
they are the thing that,

568
00:25:06.019 --> 00:25:08.079
this is the kind of logic, the doing stuff.

569
00:25:08.079 --> 00:25:11.140
The difference here being that whilst in the behavior tree

570
00:25:11.140 --> 00:25:12.680
you would typically end,

571
00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:14.579
your leaf node is typically gonna be a task

572
00:25:14.579 --> 00:25:17.079
and you're gonna be in one of those at any given time

573
00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:19.140
in the context of state tree,

574
00:25:20.079 --> 00:25:23.000
tasks can be running,

575
00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.839
you can have many tasks running simultaneously.

576
00:25:25.839 --> 00:25:30.440
The task is not the kind of the node to be in.

577
00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:32.339
The node is really our state

578
00:25:32.339 --> 00:25:36.099
and tasks can also provide output,

579
00:25:36.099 --> 00:25:37.640
which can then be used by other parts

580
00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:40.680
of the behavior tree, of the state tree, sorry.

581
00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:43.039
So a task can return a value

582
00:25:43.039 --> 00:25:45.180
which we could plug into a parameter.

583
00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:47.200
So we could be running some kind of like,

584
00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:52.200
find a nearest enemy in our reacting state.

585
00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:55.480
The task could be feeding that into a parameter

586
00:25:55.480 --> 00:26:00.440
and then that parameter is provided to our flee state

587
00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.480
so that our flee can flee from the nearest enemy.

588
00:26:05.779 --> 00:26:09.839
And I'm just coming up with a situation here,

589
00:26:09.839 --> 00:26:13.140
but that's essentially how that could hypothetically work,

590
00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:16.599
that our tasks within our reacting state

591
00:26:16.599 --> 00:26:20.160
could provide information that we bind to parameters

592
00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:21.859
and those parameters can be utilized

593
00:26:21.859 --> 00:26:23.440
by any of the elements

594
00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:25.559
beneath that reacting state in the tree.

595
00:26:27.759 --> 00:26:29.920
Yeah, common outputs might be selecting of a target,

596
00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:32.960
like I said, finding the kind of nearest enemy

597
00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:34.500
or finding the nearest target

598
00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:36.259
that you want to attack or something like that.

599
00:26:36.259 --> 00:26:37.920
Another thing might be that, you know,

600
00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:39.619
you might play an animation in the task.

601
00:26:39.619 --> 00:26:41.519
You can imagine all the kinds of different things

602
00:26:41.519 --> 00:26:43.119
that you might need to do in a behavior tree task,

603
00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:45.440
which you could also do in a state tree task,

604
00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:49.240
but it's also the idea that they can make data available

605
00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:50.539
to the rest of the tree

606
00:26:50.539 --> 00:26:52.839
and also that they can be running concurrently.

607
00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:57.440
And also when a task completes, it triggers a transition,

608
00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:59.480
which can result in the selection of the new state

609
00:26:59.480 --> 00:27:02.049
if you kind of specified that.

610
00:27:02.049 --> 00:27:03.710
There's also this idea of global tasks,

611
00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:04.890
which is worth mentioning,

612
00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:06.789
which is essentially tasks that exist

613
00:27:06.789 --> 00:27:08.609
out of particular states

614
00:27:08.609 --> 00:27:13.609
and kind of exist at the root level of the tree itself

615
00:27:13.869 --> 00:27:16.869
so they're always running as long as the tree is running.

616
00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:20.609
I'll actually kind of just quickly show that.

617
00:27:20.609 --> 00:27:22.769
We've got a state tree here,

618
00:27:22.769 --> 00:27:24.769
if I jump into our state tree example.

619
00:27:24.769 --> 00:27:26.910
Again, there is a state tree example with this project,

620
00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:28.410
so you can kind of take a look at this,

621
00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:30.970
but you can see we've got, here's our tree.

622
00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:32.230
It's a lot simpler,

623
00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:35.210
but you can see we've also got the ability.

624
00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:36.470
This is the kind of,

625
00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:39.569
whilst we're talking about individual states here,

626
00:27:39.569 --> 00:27:41.470
we can define things that are going on in these states.

627
00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:43.730
We can define tasks for this state,

628
00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:45.430
but also here we have the asset details

629
00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:48.089
for the state tree itself,

630
00:27:48.089 --> 00:27:50.130
and you can see the state tree itself,

631
00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:52.170
we can expose kind of global parameters.

632
00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:54.190
We can expose global evaluators.

633
00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:55.930
Again, we don't know what each of those things means

634
00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:56.769
at the moment,

635
00:27:56.769 --> 00:27:58.730
but there is this kind of global set of data,

636
00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:00.130
and we have global tasks,

637
00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:01.769
which are tasks that will be running

638
00:28:01.769 --> 00:28:04.490
as long as this state tree is running in any context,

639
00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:06.880
regardless of what state we're in.

640
00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:07.720
And again, yeah,

641
00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:09.319
they can make permanent data available

642
00:28:09.319 --> 00:28:12.119
for the rest of our logic for our tree,

643
00:28:12.119 --> 00:28:14.730
for selecting different states.

644
00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:16.230
Then we have transitions.

645
00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:20.230
So transitions, similar to state trees,

646
00:28:22.329 --> 00:28:24.569
to state machines, sorry.

647
00:28:26.069 --> 00:28:28.569
This is basically the rules that would govern

648
00:28:28.569 --> 00:28:30.869
when we transition from one state to another.

649
00:28:32.519 --> 00:28:35.759
And they can contain trigger conditions

650
00:28:35.759 --> 00:28:38.099
that need to be met before the transition triggers.

651
00:28:38.099 --> 00:28:40.799
So that could be, in the example you'll see in a minute,

652
00:28:40.799 --> 00:28:43.400
we've got a transition that will only occur

653
00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:45.059
when your health has reached zero,

654
00:28:45.059 --> 00:28:47.500
therefore we should transition into the dead state,

655
00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:50.940
or maybe it's that your ammo is full,

656
00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:52.460
and therefore we don't need to go be

657
00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:54.440
in the search for ammo state anymore,

658
00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:55.640
or something like this.

659
00:28:57.039 --> 00:28:59.559
Yeah, some transitions are monitored at intervals,

660
00:28:59.559 --> 00:29:02.259
whilst others are only executed when state's complete.

661
00:29:02.259 --> 00:29:04.059
So as we kind of mentioned here,

662
00:29:04.059 --> 00:29:05.799
we've got the idea of tasks

663
00:29:05.799 --> 00:29:08.900
that can trigger transitions,

664
00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:11.240
but also we can check these things on an interval.

665
00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:14.960
So for something like death, we could either,

666
00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:16.700
well, it probably makes sense to kind of check

667
00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:18.799
that on a frequency to be like, you know,

668
00:29:18.799 --> 00:29:21.140
every frame or every couple of seconds,

669
00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:22.740
check whether or not our health is zero,

670
00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:26.400
and then transition into the dead state.

671
00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:29.539
And then the completion of the transition triggers.

672
00:29:33.700 --> 00:29:34.539
Yeah, yeah.

673
00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:38.480
So essentially, the completion of transitions

674
00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:40.240
trigger the state where the task

675
00:29:40.240 --> 00:29:41.680
that is completed is located.

676
00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:45.779
So the transition completes within the,

677
00:29:45.779 --> 00:29:47.680
at the point where the task that is completing

678
00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.200
that transition is located.

679
00:29:50.799 --> 00:29:54.000
And then, yeah, the other thing to mention

680
00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.180
is we've talked about the idea of like,

681
00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.880
transitions that are triggered when states complete,

682
00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:02.799
or tasks complete, and we've also talked about the idea

683
00:30:02.839 --> 00:30:05.200
of transitions that occur on events,

684
00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:07.559
such as tick, such as on a frequency.

685
00:30:07.559 --> 00:30:10.000
It's worth mentioning that the ones that are tick

686
00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:12.900
and event-based are handled before completion transitions,

687
00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:16.440
so that if they technically happen within the same moment,

688
00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:19.500
we have the ability to execute the event,

689
00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:22.680
the ones that are responding to events first.

690
00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:24.000
And then last of all, like I said,

691
00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.039
we have this idea of evaluators and parameters.

692
00:30:27.039 --> 00:30:30.680
Essentially, both of these, these are distinct notions,

693
00:30:30.680 --> 00:30:32.440
but they achieve the same result,

694
00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:36.579
which is exposing data to the rest of our state tree.

695
00:30:36.579 --> 00:30:40.240
So parameters are essentially variables

696
00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:43.240
that can be utilized throughout the state tree.

697
00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:45.140
Parameters can either exist globally

698
00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:46.240
within the whole state tree,

699
00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:49.180
or they can exist within states.

700
00:30:49.180 --> 00:30:51.380
So again, if we just come back to our image here,

701
00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:55.339
we can have, our reacting could have parameters within it,

702
00:30:55.339 --> 00:30:57.480
which are then inherently exposed to flee,

703
00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:58.839
handle obstacle, and hit,

704
00:30:58.839 --> 00:31:01.700
or our entire state tree could have parameters in it,

705
00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:05.259
which every state can either write into

706
00:31:05.259 --> 00:31:08.799
through various tasks or read from

707
00:31:08.799 --> 00:31:11.059
for various transition logic or whatever.

708
00:31:12.259 --> 00:31:13.859
Evaluators are similar,

709
00:31:13.859 --> 00:31:16.900
but where parameters are the data that is written into,

710
00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:21.000
evaluators are essentially functions that we can run

711
00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:23.759
to go and grab data as and when we want it.

712
00:31:23.759 --> 00:31:26.660
So we've got an example of an evaluator

713
00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:28.660
in the little example that we've got,

714
00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:30.740
which is essentially just to try and find

715
00:31:30.779 --> 00:31:32.980
an appropriate spline nearby.

716
00:31:33.839 --> 00:31:36.140
But yeah, essentially they're scriptable queries

717
00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:38.079
that let us kind of get data

718
00:31:38.079 --> 00:31:40.000
in a way that isn't just relying on data

719
00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:43.579
being written into by other parts of the state tree.

720
00:31:43.579 --> 00:31:47.420
And the other thing that's worth mentioning is if we,

721
00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:49.539
I'll actually just quickly jump into the example

722
00:31:49.539 --> 00:31:50.619
to kind of show this.

723
00:31:50.619 --> 00:31:52.779
So this is what a state tree looks like.

724
00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:56.279
Like I said, we've got our own example level.

725
00:31:56.279 --> 00:31:57.880
So if you have the project downloaded,

726
00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.339
you'll be able to open this up.

727
00:32:00.339 --> 00:32:02.440
Worth mentioning as well that state tree

728
00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:04.140
is behind a plugin,

729
00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:07.640
but I think this is a plugin that is enabled by default,

730
00:32:07.640 --> 00:32:10.799
but if not, do just check that it's enabled.

731
00:32:10.799 --> 00:32:13.240
So again, this will be enabled in this project,

732
00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:15.039
but if you're doing this in your own gameplay,

733
00:32:15.039 --> 00:32:16.839
state tree and state tree,

734
00:32:16.839 --> 00:32:19.940
I think both of these come enabled by default,

735
00:32:19.940 --> 00:32:21.799
but I might be mistaken about that.

736
00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:27.079
But either way, so we've got this example level set up.

737
00:32:27.119 --> 00:32:32.119
This again is if you went to the state tree Quickstart,

738
00:32:32.619 --> 00:32:35.680
then this is going to be a very, very similar implementation.

739
00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:37.180
We're going to achieve the same goal.

740
00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:38.640
The only thing that I would say is

741
00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:40.480
some of the documentation on the Quickstart

742
00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.079
is slightly out of date

743
00:32:42.079 --> 00:32:44.720
or certain bits of data have kind of moved around

744
00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:46.380
or changed names slightly.

745
00:32:46.380 --> 00:32:49.519
So if you have any confusion or struggles

746
00:32:49.519 --> 00:32:51.380
whilst you're implementing that

747
00:32:51.380 --> 00:32:53.279
and you are following the documentation,

748
00:32:53.279 --> 00:32:54.880
maybe just look at this as an example

749
00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:59.380
for what might have been done incorrectly.

750
00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.700
So what we've got is essentially a state tree

751
00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:06.299
that is achieving some sort of AI.

752
00:33:06.299 --> 00:33:09.880
And in this case, it's going to be a kind of target

753
00:33:09.880 --> 00:33:11.500
that's going to move around the spline

754
00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:14.740
and is going to respond to basically when it's dead,

755
00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:17.480
it's going to kind of transition into a dead state.

756
00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:18.640
So as soon as I hit play,

757
00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:20.480
you'll see I've just got this target

758
00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:22.539
that kind of loops around on a spline

759
00:33:22.539 --> 00:33:25.079
and it will kind of like just repeatedly loop.

760
00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:27.339
I've also got like a little cursor

761
00:33:27.339 --> 00:33:28.180
in the middle of the screen.

762
00:33:28.180 --> 00:33:29.240
This isn't to do with the state tree,

763
00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:30.140
but this is just purely

764
00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:33.200
so I can actually see what I'm doing.

765
00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:34.839
So what happens is when I click,

766
00:33:34.839 --> 00:33:36.140
I'm doing a ray trace

767
00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:38.779
for whether or not it's an enemy object.

768
00:33:38.779 --> 00:33:39.880
And if it's the enemy object,

769
00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:41.339
I'm going to damage that enemy.

770
00:33:41.339 --> 00:33:43.380
And when I shoot my enemy three times,

771
00:33:43.380 --> 00:33:45.039
then it's going to transition into our dead state

772
00:33:45.039 --> 00:33:47.640
and it's going to kind of show some debugging behavior

773
00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:49.539
just to kind of represent that.

774
00:33:49.539 --> 00:33:51.440
So if I click on the enemy one time,

775
00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:54.039
two times, three times, now it's dead.

776
00:33:54.039 --> 00:33:55.380
And you can see we get this little debug

777
00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:57.680
to say actor destroyed, and then it's gone.

778
00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.880
And that is all utilizing the state tree.

779
00:34:00.799 --> 00:34:02.880
So one thing that kind of, like I said,

780
00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:06.299
this acts as a kind of replacement to behavior trees.

781
00:34:07.240 --> 00:34:08.800
But one of the things that's kind of really nice

782
00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:12.099
about the behavior tree is how easy it is to debug.

783
00:34:12.099 --> 00:34:13.599
And one of the things you might notice

784
00:34:13.599 --> 00:34:15.599
whilst we're playing is there isn't,

785
00:34:15.599 --> 00:34:17.539
doesn't seem to be like an innate way.

786
00:34:17.539 --> 00:34:20.139
There doesn't seem to be quite that same plugin

787
00:34:20.139 --> 00:34:23.380
and play logic for debugging

788
00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:25.139
what's currently going on in your state tree.

789
00:34:25.139 --> 00:34:27.539
If I were to kind of quickly play this,

790
00:34:27.539 --> 00:34:29.340
you see I'm not getting this kind of,

791
00:34:29.340 --> 00:34:30.980
I don't have that same thing that I do

792
00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:34.739
with our behavior tree where I've got those kind

793
00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:35.980
of chevrons where I can drop down

794
00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:38.099
and choose which behavior tree I'm listening to

795
00:34:38.099 --> 00:34:40.800
and then see a runtime representation of what's going on.

796
00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:42.780
Or it can seem that way initially.

797
00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:45.940
Actually, there is a debugger.

798
00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:48.099
It's not kind of put right to the front

799
00:34:48.139 --> 00:34:50.440
and it's not quite as instantaneously available,

800
00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:51.460
but it is there.

801
00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:54.559
And it's essentially an implementation of what,

802
00:34:54.559 --> 00:34:58.840
of a more of kind of a whole subset

803
00:34:58.840 --> 00:35:00.739
of Unreal debugging functionality,

804
00:35:00.739 --> 00:35:02.639
which is called the visual logger.

805
00:35:02.639 --> 00:35:06.199
The visual logger is essentially a tool

806
00:35:06.199 --> 00:35:08.800
that can record state about your world

807
00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:10.900
and then gives you the ability to kind of,

808
00:35:10.900 --> 00:35:13.659
and it can write, it doesn't just log data,

809
00:35:13.659 --> 00:35:16.099
but it logs data in a kind of visual way.

810
00:35:16.139 --> 00:35:19.139
So you could, for example, log down the positions of things

811
00:35:19.139 --> 00:35:22.179
and you could actually see that data visualized

812
00:35:22.179 --> 00:35:23.300
in your level.

813
00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:24.659
And the other thing about the visual logger

814
00:35:24.659 --> 00:35:27.539
that's very cool is you kind of record your world state

815
00:35:27.539 --> 00:35:30.340
and you can actually scrub back through that state

816
00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:33.000
in a timeline to kind of track where

817
00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:36.539
and when things changed and really visualize these things

818
00:35:36.539 --> 00:35:39.260
in a way that isn't always baked into that like,

819
00:35:39.260 --> 00:35:40.940
oh, I've just missed it this frame

820
00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:42.239
and now it's gone forever kind of thing.

821
00:35:42.239 --> 00:35:43.619
I have to kind of replicate it

822
00:35:43.619 --> 00:35:45.179
and hopefully put a break point.

823
00:35:45.179 --> 00:35:48.860
It's done in this kind of saved a timeline scrubable way,

824
00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:49.880
which is very nice.

825
00:35:51.059 --> 00:35:52.900
So if you're familiar with the visual logger,

826
00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:55.000
then this is gonna look very similar.

827
00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:57.219
You'll be kind of quite familiar with this,

828
00:35:57.219 --> 00:36:00.059
but if you just, once you have the state tree open,

829
00:36:00.059 --> 00:36:02.300
so state trees are represented in their own assets,

830
00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:04.880
which is in the example is just here,

831
00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:06.260
we have the state tree asset.

832
00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:11.260
If you just go to, I think it's window and then debugger

833
00:36:11.260 --> 00:36:14.360
that opens the state tree debugger.

834
00:36:14.360 --> 00:36:17.380
And if you are just playing,

835
00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:19.880
this has actually got a previous recording in there,

836
00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:21.320
but if I just went to,

837
00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:25.670
if I just snap this to the side of my screen

838
00:36:25.670 --> 00:36:30.940
and then played with that debugger window open,

839
00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:31.780
you would see it's actually,

840
00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:34.059
you can see it recording data.

841
00:36:34.059 --> 00:36:37.900
And then I'm just gonna do the shooting

842
00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:39.039
and now my actor is destroyed.

843
00:36:39.039 --> 00:36:42.780
And you can see it's clearly responded to that

844
00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:43.860
and it stopped recording

845
00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:46.539
because the whole state tree is now closed off.

846
00:36:47.679 --> 00:36:49.980
So I'll just stop the recording because I can,

847
00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:52.860
but we can now,

848
00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:55.440
so whilst we're actually playing,

849
00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:56.760
you'll see whilst the state tree is running,

850
00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:58.940
you do actually have a live representation

851
00:36:58.940 --> 00:36:59.780
of what's going on.

852
00:36:59.780 --> 00:37:01.079
You can see this is actually showing me

853
00:37:01.079 --> 00:37:04.059
I'm in my moving spline state.

854
00:37:04.059 --> 00:37:05.780
And if I take enough damage,

855
00:37:05.780 --> 00:37:07.340
then it transitions into the dead state.

856
00:37:07.340 --> 00:37:09.280
You can see all of this stuff happening.

857
00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:10.260
But as well as that,

858
00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:13.360
if I stop that recording and I'm scrubbing through it,

859
00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:15.880
you can see I have this now saved representation

860
00:37:15.880 --> 00:37:18.170
of all the things that went on.

861
00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:19.690
And again, some of this will be familiar

862
00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:22.329
from the, if you've used the visual logger before,

863
00:37:22.329 --> 00:37:25.090
but you can actually see bits of information

864
00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:26.269
that are getting debugged here

865
00:37:26.269 --> 00:37:27.809
about like what state we're in,

866
00:37:27.809 --> 00:37:30.690
what transitions were being queried

867
00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:32.809
and all of this information.

868
00:37:32.809 --> 00:37:35.889
So it's kind of a lot of very, very helpful information.

869
00:37:37.449 --> 00:37:40.250
And yes, that's kind of like an implementation

870
00:37:40.250 --> 00:37:41.090
of the visual logger.

871
00:37:41.090 --> 00:37:42.670
So just worth bearing in mind

872
00:37:42.670 --> 00:37:44.710
that when you're working with state tree,

873
00:37:45.829 --> 00:37:49.949
it's not immediately obvious that you can debug it,

874
00:37:49.949 --> 00:37:52.150
but you can, and it's very, very powerful.

875
00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:55.739
Yeah, and I suppose if you want to go

876
00:37:55.739 --> 00:37:56.739
into more information

877
00:37:56.739 --> 00:37:59.179
about this kind of particular implementation,

878
00:37:59.179 --> 00:38:01.059
again, this is essentially just a re-implementation

879
00:38:01.059 --> 00:38:03.260
of the quick start.

880
00:38:03.260 --> 00:38:04.679
So you can kind of follow up there.

881
00:38:04.679 --> 00:38:08.179
I'm not gonna go into too much more detail about this,

882
00:38:08.179 --> 00:38:10.059
but you can look at the project

883
00:38:10.059 --> 00:38:16.380
and look at the documentation.

884
00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:18.099
Next up, we have mass entity.

885
00:38:18.099 --> 00:38:20.400
This is a little bit like the other plugins,

886
00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:24.239
isn't strictly related to AI,

887
00:38:24.239 --> 00:38:28.340
but it definitely has relevance.

888
00:38:28.340 --> 00:38:33.159
So if you've watched some of the previous presentations,

889
00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:36.900
then you might recall all the way back

890
00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:38.400
in probably the first presentation,

891
00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:40.900
we talked about the idea of actors and components.

892
00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:42.179
And what I mentioned then

893
00:38:42.219 --> 00:38:46.880
is this is not entity component system architecture.

894
00:38:46.880 --> 00:38:49.260
Actors and components is more about the idea

895
00:38:49.260 --> 00:38:51.340
of modular reusable functionality

896
00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:53.559
that you can kind of slot into an entity.

897
00:38:53.559 --> 00:38:58.019
Mass entity is entity component system architecture.

898
00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:04.300
So mass entity is Unreal's implementation of ECS.

899
00:39:04.300 --> 00:39:06.199
So if you're familiar with ECS,

900
00:39:06.199 --> 00:39:09.239
this will already be very, very familiar to you.

901
00:39:09.239 --> 00:39:12.079
We're just going to kind of go a little bit

902
00:39:12.079 --> 00:39:14.159
through what Unreal's implementation

903
00:39:14.159 --> 00:39:15.760
of those things looks like.

904
00:39:15.760 --> 00:39:18.739
But if you're not familiar with ECS and why it exists,

905
00:39:20.699 --> 00:39:22.860
to kind of very, very briefly summarize that,

906
00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:27.019
essentially when you're working in software,

907
00:39:27.019 --> 00:39:29.760
particularly in object oriented software,

908
00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:32.119
there are a lot of things that we do as programmers

909
00:39:32.119 --> 00:39:34.699
that kind of make sense to us because we're human

910
00:39:34.699 --> 00:39:36.260
and we want to work with things in a way

911
00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:38.099
that makes sense to human beings.

912
00:39:38.539 --> 00:39:40.980
That's very good for readability.

913
00:39:40.980 --> 00:39:43.460
It's very good for maintainability.

914
00:39:43.460 --> 00:39:44.900
But what it's not always good for

915
00:39:44.900 --> 00:39:47.659
is the computer's actual performance.

916
00:39:47.659 --> 00:39:49.099
Just taking, for an example,

917
00:39:49.099 --> 00:39:52.059
a standard like enemy structure that you might have,

918
00:39:52.059 --> 00:39:53.739
you might have an enemy class

919
00:39:53.739 --> 00:39:57.659
and that enemy has a location, it has health,

920
00:39:57.659 --> 00:40:01.829
it has a mesh, it has a texture.

921
00:40:01.829 --> 00:40:04.190
And then maybe you make a child class of that

922
00:40:04.909 --> 00:40:08.190
that is like winged flying enemy

923
00:40:08.190 --> 00:40:09.789
and it's got all these extra functions

924
00:40:09.789 --> 00:40:11.869
for take flight or land

925
00:40:11.869 --> 00:40:13.710
and it brings in some additional meshes

926
00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:14.690
and stuff like that.

927
00:40:14.690 --> 00:40:18.610
And this is obviously a kind of slightly peculiar hierarchy

928
00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:20.110
that I've just constructed in my head,

929
00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:23.349
but you can imagine that we do these kinds of things.

930
00:40:23.349 --> 00:40:24.590
And so it makes a lot of sense

931
00:40:24.590 --> 00:40:27.989
to us as software engineers to have this structure

932
00:40:27.989 --> 00:40:31.269
because you can see that every winged enemy is an enemy

933
00:40:31.269 --> 00:40:33.590
and all these things are coupled together.

934
00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:35.909
The difficulty is that when you're working

935
00:40:35.909 --> 00:40:37.510
in something like a game engine,

936
00:40:38.469 --> 00:40:42.309
very commonly the game engine is working

937
00:40:42.309 --> 00:40:44.949
with lots of these things simultaneously.

938
00:40:44.949 --> 00:40:49.949
So the physics system might be iterating

939
00:40:51.170 --> 00:40:53.590
through all of the entities in your world

940
00:40:53.590 --> 00:40:55.730
to grab their collision data.

941
00:40:55.730 --> 00:41:00.730
And then when one of your characters moves,

942
00:41:00.949 --> 00:41:03.190
then we need to query all of that collision data

943
00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:06.630
to establish, am I gonna hit something?

944
00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:09.030
Just as a kind of small example.

945
00:41:09.030 --> 00:41:11.510
So if we have all of our data

946
00:41:11.510 --> 00:41:13.269
structured together in this way,

947
00:41:13.269 --> 00:41:15.070
essentially when we create that enemy

948
00:41:15.070 --> 00:41:16.469
or that winged enemy,

949
00:41:16.469 --> 00:41:20.670
we're going to hopefully block out a big chunk of memory

950
00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:22.550
that represents that enemy

951
00:41:22.550 --> 00:41:24.869
and that's gonna have its location in it.

952
00:41:24.869 --> 00:41:26.590
So that's a vector three,

953
00:41:26.590 --> 00:41:27.750
so that's gonna be what,

954
00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:31.780
like at least 96,

955
00:41:33.619 --> 00:41:35.300
so that's 96 bytes.

956
00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:37.219
And then we've got like our health,

957
00:41:37.219 --> 00:41:38.059
that's gonna be a float,

958
00:41:38.059 --> 00:41:40.699
that's gonna be at least four bytes.

959
00:41:40.699 --> 00:41:45.699
Sorry, I said 96 bits, so 12 bytes.

960
00:41:45.739 --> 00:41:46.639
And then you've got your health,

961
00:41:46.639 --> 00:41:48.139
which is gonna be another four bytes,

962
00:41:48.139 --> 00:41:49.619
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

963
00:41:49.619 --> 00:41:52.340
When our physics system is now trying to gather

964
00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:53.980
all of that data to get together,

965
00:41:53.980 --> 00:41:55.699
all the physics system really cares about

966
00:41:55.699 --> 00:41:56.780
is our collision geometry.

967
00:41:56.780 --> 00:42:00.579
And there's all this fluff in the way, right?

968
00:42:00.619 --> 00:42:03.099
It's suddenly having to,

969
00:42:03.099 --> 00:42:05.260
it's technically when it's gathering up all our enemies,

970
00:42:05.260 --> 00:42:06.860
it's getting all of those enemies' health,

971
00:42:06.860 --> 00:42:10.460
all of those enemies' positions,

972
00:42:10.460 --> 00:42:13.659
all of the extra bits of data that enemy has,

973
00:42:13.659 --> 00:42:15.139
its damage, what weapon it has equipped

974
00:42:15.139 --> 00:42:17.030
and all this kind of stuff.

975
00:42:17.030 --> 00:42:18.150
If you're familiar with the way

976
00:42:18.150 --> 00:42:19.710
that CPUs are structured,

977
00:42:19.710 --> 00:42:20.989
you would be familiar with the idea

978
00:42:20.989 --> 00:42:22.550
that we have this concept of caches.

979
00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:25.469
We have an L1 cache, an L2 cache, L3 cache,

980
00:42:25.469 --> 00:42:26.550
sometimes L4 cache,

981
00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:29.150
and then we go up to random access memory

982
00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:30.909
to our computer's RAM.

983
00:42:31.869 --> 00:42:35.070
So, and each of these caches and RAM

984
00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:36.750
perform at different speeds.

985
00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:40.409
So for the best performance,

986
00:42:40.409 --> 00:42:43.550
essentially what our computer would like,

987
00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:45.949
would be best for the CPU performance

988
00:42:45.949 --> 00:42:48.710
is to be always getting data from my L1 cache.

989
00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:50.309
Our L1 cache is our smallest,

990
00:42:50.309 --> 00:42:52.929
but most high performance section of memory.

991
00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:59.110
Whenever we try and get data

992
00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:01.030
that is not in our L1 cache,

993
00:43:01.030 --> 00:43:03.110
it will kind of look up to our L2 cache

994
00:43:03.110 --> 00:43:04.550
to see if it's there,

995
00:43:04.550 --> 00:43:05.909
and if it's there, then fine,

996
00:43:05.909 --> 00:43:08.110
that's gonna be a little bit slower, but that's okay.

997
00:43:08.110 --> 00:43:10.630
And so on and so forth.

998
00:43:10.630 --> 00:43:11.989
So if it's not in our L2 cache,

999
00:43:11.989 --> 00:43:13.429
it'll look through our L3 cache,

1000
00:43:13.429 --> 00:43:16.190
and eventually it ends up at RAM.

1001
00:43:16.190 --> 00:43:17.829
And each of these steps,

1002
00:43:17.829 --> 00:43:19.309
getting data from our L2 cache,

1003
00:43:19.309 --> 00:43:20.989
getting data from our L3 cache,

1004
00:43:20.989 --> 00:43:22.389
getting data from our RAM

1005
00:43:22.389 --> 00:43:25.989
is gonna be exponentially more expensive

1006
00:43:25.989 --> 00:43:27.050
in terms of time cost,

1007
00:43:27.050 --> 00:43:28.809
in terms of actual CPU cycles,

1008
00:43:29.369 --> 00:43:30.929
so the time it takes to literally get that memory

1009
00:43:30.929 --> 00:43:34.670
from that place to our CPU for operating on.

1010
00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:36.389
Whenever our CPU does that,

1011
00:43:36.389 --> 00:43:38.309
whenever our CPU gets data,

1012
00:43:38.309 --> 00:43:39.769
what it will do is essentially get something

1013
00:43:39.769 --> 00:43:41.469
called a cache line.

1014
00:43:41.469 --> 00:43:43.469
It doesn't just get the data it's asking for.

1015
00:43:43.469 --> 00:43:47.190
It typically gets a chunk of data

1016
00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:49.469
to either side of that memory

1017
00:43:50.389 --> 00:43:52.949
because it's gathering that on a bus.

1018
00:43:52.949 --> 00:43:54.309
That bus is a particular size.

1019
00:43:54.309 --> 00:43:56.630
It might as well gather that much memory.

1020
00:43:56.670 --> 00:44:01.150
And then if the next bit of data it reads

1021
00:44:01.150 --> 00:44:03.789
happens to be within that same cache line,

1022
00:44:03.789 --> 00:44:05.170
that's basically a free read.

1023
00:44:05.170 --> 00:44:07.150
That memory is already available to you.

1024
00:44:07.150 --> 00:44:09.610
It's like whenever our computer is,

1025
00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:11.710
the term bus is quite applicable here,

1026
00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:15.389
it's whenever our computer is going out to get memory,

1027
00:44:15.389 --> 00:44:17.829
it's loading all of that memory onto a bus,

1028
00:44:17.829 --> 00:44:19.269
and anything that's already on that bus

1029
00:44:19.269 --> 00:44:20.710
is already there, right?

1030
00:44:20.710 --> 00:44:22.869
So the more that we can compact our data

1031
00:44:22.869 --> 00:44:24.170
in a particular way,

1032
00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:27.329
the more when we're doing certain bits of functionality,

1033
00:44:27.329 --> 00:44:29.969
if we can kind of try and align that data

1034
00:44:29.969 --> 00:44:33.090
so that we know whatever data we're gonna need next

1035
00:44:33.090 --> 00:44:34.809
is next to that data,

1036
00:44:34.809 --> 00:44:36.690
it vastly increases the chance

1037
00:44:36.690 --> 00:44:38.409
that we'll be caught in the same cache line

1038
00:44:38.409 --> 00:44:40.210
and certainly increases the chance

1039
00:44:40.210 --> 00:44:42.570
that it's gonna be part of the same,

1040
00:44:42.570 --> 00:44:45.010
at least in the L2 cache, at least in the L3 cache,

1041
00:44:45.010 --> 00:44:47.369
that all of the most relevant data

1042
00:44:47.369 --> 00:44:48.730
is gonna be grouped together

1043
00:44:48.730 --> 00:44:51.130
and all of the bits of the data that aren't relevant

1044
00:44:51.130 --> 00:44:53.849
are gonna be kind of like not considered.

1045
00:44:54.289 --> 00:44:55.130
That's essentially the problem

1046
00:44:55.130 --> 00:44:56.889
that ECS architecture is there to solve.

1047
00:44:56.889 --> 00:45:01.889
It's a way of grouping only the bits of relevant data in,

1048
00:45:01.889 --> 00:45:04.929
well, that's what our component is,

1049
00:45:04.929 --> 00:45:06.010
and then we have the NC

1050
00:45:06.010 --> 00:45:07.489
that kind of groups these components together

1051
00:45:07.489 --> 00:45:09.929
and the systems that work with the components.

1052
00:45:09.929 --> 00:45:11.630
And so the system only cares

1053
00:45:11.630 --> 00:45:13.670
about the isolated bits of data.

1054
00:45:13.670 --> 00:45:15.550
We can ram all of these

1055
00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:17.409
compacted as possible bits of data together,

1056
00:45:17.409 --> 00:45:20.980
and therefore the system can be as performant as possible.

1057
00:45:20.980 --> 00:45:22.639
So that was quite a lot of,

1058
00:45:22.639 --> 00:45:25.920
you know, jargon and words.

1059
00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:28.519
And for anyone who's kind of more an expert

1060
00:45:28.519 --> 00:45:31.659
on CPUs and ECS architecture,

1061
00:45:31.659 --> 00:45:33.619
I may have, you know, made some mistakes there,

1062
00:45:33.619 --> 00:45:35.780
but that's broadly the problem it's trying to achieve.

1063
00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:37.159
So that's the reason that you,

1064
00:45:37.159 --> 00:45:39.440
essentially ECS architecture

1065
00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:41.119
is something that you may consider using

1066
00:45:41.119 --> 00:45:43.760
when you have highly performant bits of functionality

1067
00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:45.559
that you need, where you need lots of data

1068
00:45:45.559 --> 00:45:47.880
and you need to do something with that data.

1069
00:45:47.880 --> 00:45:51.599
So Boyd's implementations might be an example,

1070
00:45:51.599 --> 00:45:53.519
crowds, traffic.

1071
00:45:53.519 --> 00:45:56.960
So yeah, anything where you're doing a lot of something

1072
00:45:57.880 --> 00:45:59.880
and you can kind of boil that something down

1073
00:45:59.880 --> 00:46:02.840
into kind of not exactly atomic,

1074
00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:07.079
but small bits of data that are highly reusable,

1075
00:46:07.079 --> 00:46:08.639
that might be something where you might want

1076
00:46:08.639 --> 00:46:10.000
to consider ECS architecture.

1077
00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:12.440
And mass entity is essentially

1078
00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:15.019
Unreal's implementation of ECS.

1079
00:46:15.019 --> 00:46:17.059
So then basically what we have

1080
00:46:17.059 --> 00:46:20.260
is just a mapping of terms, right?

1081
00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:21.820
If you're familiar with ECS,

1082
00:46:22.659 --> 00:46:25.539
then fragment is essentially our component.

1083
00:46:25.539 --> 00:46:27.619
It represents an atomic piece of data

1084
00:46:27.619 --> 00:46:29.340
used in the calculations.

1085
00:46:29.340 --> 00:46:32.579
So examples of this might be a transform or a velocity.

1086
00:46:32.579 --> 00:46:34.780
And again, these fragments are data only.

1087
00:46:34.780 --> 00:46:38.380
They aren't part of the point of ECS architecture

1088
00:46:38.380 --> 00:46:39.820
is you are not marrying up,

1089
00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:44.820
unlike typical object-orientated functionality

1090
00:46:45.579 --> 00:46:47.099
where you marry up,

1091
00:46:47.099 --> 00:46:50.860
a lot of the time you marry up data with functionality.

1092
00:46:50.860 --> 00:46:53.699
Fragments are there only to provide the data,

1093
00:46:53.699 --> 00:46:55.420
only to provide the memory.

1094
00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:57.139
Fragments also can't be created in blueprint,

1095
00:46:57.139 --> 00:46:58.059
which is something to bear in mind.

1096
00:46:58.059 --> 00:47:02.219
A lot of this is kind of more of a C plus plus thing.

1097
00:47:02.219 --> 00:47:03.380
Then we have entities,

1098
00:47:03.380 --> 00:47:05.260
which the term is the same,

1099
00:47:05.260 --> 00:47:08.380
and this is maps to our entity and our ECS architecture.

1100
00:47:08.380 --> 00:47:09.699
And essentially entities

1101
00:47:09.699 --> 00:47:11.420
represent collections of fragments.

1102
00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:17.000
So these kind of group things,

1103
00:47:18.119 --> 00:47:19.440
group these fragments together.

1104
00:47:19.480 --> 00:47:20.320
So when our,

1105
00:47:22.900 --> 00:47:25.219
yeah, they kind of group our fragments together

1106
00:47:25.219 --> 00:47:26.900
in a possibly unique way.

1107
00:47:26.900 --> 00:47:30.139
So they contain a unique identifier

1108
00:47:30.139 --> 00:47:31.179
that could be used for lookups

1109
00:47:31.179 --> 00:47:33.099
so that we kind of know which entity

1110
00:47:33.099 --> 00:47:33.980
we're talking about

1111
00:47:33.980 --> 00:47:37.429
when we're talking about particular archetypes.

1112
00:47:37.429 --> 00:47:40.909
And yeah, creating an entity is similar to class instancing,

1113
00:47:43.329 --> 00:47:47.969
but the way entities actually are defined

1114
00:47:47.969 --> 00:47:51.050
is really all an entity kind of needs to know about

1115
00:47:51.369 --> 00:47:54.809
other than the individual instances of that entity.

1116
00:47:54.809 --> 00:47:57.530
Part of the entity's definition

1117
00:47:57.530 --> 00:47:59.449
is what fragments it's made up of.

1118
00:48:00.289 --> 00:48:03.329
And an entity's composition

1119
00:48:03.329 --> 00:48:05.809
is very relevant to the rest of the system

1120
00:48:05.809 --> 00:48:08.369
because we're gonna get into this in a moment,

1121
00:48:08.369 --> 00:48:09.570
this concept of archetypes,

1122
00:48:09.570 --> 00:48:10.610
which are essentially groups

1123
00:48:10.610 --> 00:48:12.570
and see the same composition together.

1124
00:48:12.570 --> 00:48:15.409
Compositions of entities can be changed at runtime

1125
00:48:16.369 --> 00:48:20.929
so that they can kind of gain fragments.

1126
00:48:21.650 --> 00:48:23.489
If that's required.

1127
00:48:23.489 --> 00:48:27.730
But yeah, essentially entities exist to group

1128
00:48:28.969 --> 00:48:30.010
sets of fragments together

1129
00:48:30.010 --> 00:48:33.090
for whatever this entity kind of requires

1130
00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:34.969
and can also be uniquely looked up.

1131
00:48:34.969 --> 00:48:36.769
They have an entity ID

1132
00:48:36.769 --> 00:48:39.050
to kind of pair a particular fragment up

1133
00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:41.409
with what entity is this for.

1134
00:48:41.409 --> 00:48:43.369
Similar to fragments, these are data only,

1135
00:48:43.369 --> 00:48:45.610
so they shouldn't have any kind of functions in them.

1136
00:48:45.610 --> 00:48:47.889
It is pure data.

1137
00:48:47.889 --> 00:48:49.010
So then we have archetypes,

1138
00:48:49.010 --> 00:48:51.849
which don't necessarily,

1139
00:48:51.849 --> 00:48:53.289
they certainly don't have a marry up

1140
00:48:53.289 --> 00:48:55.730
to any of the EC or S,

1141
00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:58.610
but archetypes are essentially collections of entities

1142
00:48:58.610 --> 00:49:00.170
with identical composition.

1143
00:49:01.250 --> 00:49:05.130
And it's these archetypes that are utilized

1144
00:49:05.130 --> 00:49:09.889
when our processes, which is our S, our system,

1145
00:49:09.889 --> 00:49:12.289
when our systems try to work with things,

1146
00:49:12.289 --> 00:49:14.210
they actually, what they will query for

1147
00:49:14.210 --> 00:49:16.210
is archetypes for all entities

1148
00:49:16.210 --> 00:49:17.769
that have the same composition.

1149
00:49:21.619 --> 00:49:23.699
Yeah, so an archetype is in a way

1150
00:49:23.699 --> 00:49:26.099
that an entity of a particular composition

1151
00:49:26.099 --> 00:49:28.539
and an archetype is composed of different types

1152
00:49:28.539 --> 00:49:32.429
of fragments in a specific configuration.

1153
00:49:32.429 --> 00:49:35.230
So for an example of an archetype might be this,

1154
00:49:35.230 --> 00:49:36.949
an archetype is going to be,

1155
00:49:36.949 --> 00:49:39.150
our archetype is transform velocity,

1156
00:49:39.150 --> 00:49:42.469
and therefore it's going to contain an array

1157
00:49:42.469 --> 00:49:45.750
of all the entities that have a composition

1158
00:49:45.750 --> 00:49:48.909
that is a transform fragment

1159
00:49:48.909 --> 00:49:51.590
and a velocity vector fragment.

1160
00:49:52.110 --> 00:49:56.230
And then we're going to have this array of entities here.

1161
00:49:56.230 --> 00:49:57.869
We've got our entity ID here.

1162
00:49:57.869 --> 00:50:00.949
So, you know, zero, one or two or whatever.

1163
00:50:00.949 --> 00:50:03.030
And then here is the velocity for each,

1164
00:50:03.030 --> 00:50:08.030
is the velocity fragment for entity zero.

1165
00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:11.269
Here's our transform fragment for entity zero

1166
00:50:11.269 --> 00:50:13.309
and so on and so forth for entity one,

1167
00:50:13.309 --> 00:50:16.099
entity two, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

1168
00:50:16.099 --> 00:50:18.780
So you can see how these things kind of couple together.

1169
00:50:18.780 --> 00:50:21.860
Our archetype is all of the entities

1170
00:50:21.860 --> 00:50:23.059
of the same composition,

1171
00:50:23.059 --> 00:50:24.940
composition here being entities

1172
00:50:24.940 --> 00:50:27.019
that have only a transform fragment

1173
00:50:27.019 --> 00:50:30.300
and only a transform and velocity fragment.

1174
00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:32.820
And then we'll contain them in an array.

1175
00:50:32.820 --> 00:50:35.739
And then these things are kind of like paired up.

1176
00:50:35.739 --> 00:50:39.579
So the ID is paired up with its transform

1177
00:50:39.579 --> 00:50:41.769
and its velocity.

1178
00:50:41.769 --> 00:50:45.889
So processes is the S of our ECS architecture, our systems.

1179
00:50:45.889 --> 00:50:47.329
And these are essentially the things

1180
00:50:47.329 --> 00:50:51.090
where entity and fragment are data only.

1181
00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:52.809
Processor is the thing that actually does

1182
00:50:52.809 --> 00:50:54.050
the functionality.

1183
00:50:54.050 --> 00:50:57.449
And the kind of one of the key concepts about processes

1184
00:50:57.449 --> 00:50:59.409
is they are stateless.

1185
00:50:59.409 --> 00:51:02.170
So they are really just there to do things

1186
00:51:02.170 --> 00:51:05.889
to the fragments and entity which provide the data.

1187
00:51:07.210 --> 00:51:10.329
And essentially, as we were kind of already mentioned,

1188
00:51:10.329 --> 00:51:13.409
oh no, actually I can display that in the next segment,

1189
00:51:13.409 --> 00:51:17.250
but they use entity queries to specify

1190
00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:19.210
what kind of archetypes they're looking for,

1191
00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:20.690
for what kind of fragments they need.

1192
00:51:20.690 --> 00:51:24.170
And then they get this NC query will return an archetype

1193
00:51:24.170 --> 00:51:26.530
which contains all the relevant fragments

1194
00:51:26.530 --> 00:51:29.929
for all the operations that this system would need to do.

1195
00:51:29.929 --> 00:51:32.530
So a system might be something

1196
00:51:32.530 --> 00:51:33.889
like a traffic management system.

1197
00:51:33.889 --> 00:51:36.969
And therefore it only needs to know the locations

1198
00:51:36.969 --> 00:51:40.889
and velocities of all of the traffic.

1199
00:51:40.889 --> 00:51:42.489
And therefore it makes a query saying,

1200
00:51:42.489 --> 00:51:44.650
I need an archetype that is anything

1201
00:51:44.650 --> 00:51:47.449
that has a transform and velocity.

1202
00:51:47.449 --> 00:51:50.730
And then the query will return that archetype

1203
00:51:50.730 --> 00:51:52.050
with all the relevant entities.

1204
00:51:52.050 --> 00:51:54.329
And then the traffic system just performs

1205
00:51:54.329 --> 00:51:57.170
whatever operations it needs to on those entities,

1206
00:51:57.170 --> 00:51:58.730
just knowing that all I care about

1207
00:51:58.730 --> 00:52:00.369
is the location and the velocity.

1208
00:52:00.369 --> 00:52:02.769
And I add the velocity to the transform

1209
00:52:02.769 --> 00:52:05.340
or whatever the system is gonna do.

1210
00:52:05.340 --> 00:52:08.579
An example of this could be the mass movement processes.

1211
00:52:08.579 --> 00:52:12.230
So you can just look at that in the C++.

1212
00:52:12.269 --> 00:52:14.510
So again, kind of just a visual representation

1213
00:52:14.510 --> 00:52:15.949
of what I was talking about.

1214
00:52:15.949 --> 00:52:18.550
The processor says, I need,

1215
00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:21.670
it uses an NC query for a particular archetype

1216
00:52:21.670 --> 00:52:23.550
and the archetype it's searching for

1217
00:52:23.550 --> 00:52:25.869
is anything with a transform and velocity.

1218
00:52:25.869 --> 00:52:30.869
We filter the archetype to essentially find

1219
00:52:31.349 --> 00:52:33.190
the relevant archetype.

1220
00:52:33.190 --> 00:52:37.710
And then that's gonna return this array of entity,

1221
00:52:37.710 --> 00:52:41.110
of fragments essentially to the processor for working with

1222
00:52:41.150 --> 00:52:45.150
and that's gonna return an array of the transform

1223
00:52:45.150 --> 00:52:49.980
and velocity arrays for the person to work with.

1224
00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:51.860
Then the last thing that is worth mentioning

1225
00:52:51.860 --> 00:52:54.539
is there are a couple of special types of fragments.

1226
00:52:54.539 --> 00:52:56.860
So the examples we've given so far

1227
00:52:56.860 --> 00:52:59.900
is things like transforms, velocity, vectors,

1228
00:52:59.900 --> 00:53:01.500
you can kind of imagine,

1229
00:53:01.500 --> 00:53:04.260
but there are these extra special kind of fragment types,

1230
00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:05.539
which are worth mentioning.

1231
00:53:05.539 --> 00:53:08.260
So we have tag fragments,

1232
00:53:08.260 --> 00:53:10.820
which is a little bit like,

1233
00:53:10.860 --> 00:53:12.340
somewhat similar to gameplay tags.

1234
00:53:12.340 --> 00:53:15.260
You're familiar with that concept.

1235
00:53:15.260 --> 00:53:20.099
And the tag is a fragment which contains no data itself.

1236
00:53:20.099 --> 00:53:23.980
It is purely the presence or absence of a tag

1237
00:53:23.980 --> 00:53:25.539
that is used to represent the thing.

1238
00:53:25.539 --> 00:53:28.659
So if you could imagine something that might be like

1239
00:53:28.659 --> 00:53:30.380
an is visible flag,

1240
00:53:32.480 --> 00:53:33.880
all we need to do is have a tag

1241
00:53:33.880 --> 00:53:36.159
that is like the visible tag.

1242
00:53:36.159 --> 00:53:38.719
If that visible tag is present, then it is visible.

1243
00:53:39.400 --> 00:53:42.199
If that fragment doesn't exist at all,

1244
00:53:42.199 --> 00:53:45.760
then we can perceive it as not visible.

1245
00:53:45.760 --> 00:53:50.760
And so therefore it doesn't need any extra data.

1246
00:53:51.159 --> 00:53:52.920
It just needs to exist or not exist

1247
00:53:52.920 --> 00:53:57.920
to kind of demark that particular data about the entity.

1248
00:53:58.920 --> 00:54:01.199
It's worth mentioning that when we're adding these tags,

1249
00:54:01.199 --> 00:54:03.039
this is part of the entity's composition.

1250
00:54:03.039 --> 00:54:04.679
So when we said about archetypes,

1251
00:54:04.679 --> 00:54:07.679
grouping together all entities of the same composition,

1252
00:54:08.639 --> 00:54:10.000
they would also group together.

1253
00:54:10.000 --> 00:54:11.880
Tags would be taken into account in this.

1254
00:54:11.880 --> 00:54:14.719
So they would be grouped under a separate archetype.

1255
00:54:15.639 --> 00:54:20.639
If we had 10 entities with a transform and a velocity,

1256
00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:24.920
and we had one entity with a transform velocity and a tag,

1257
00:54:24.920 --> 00:54:27.159
then those would be in separate archetypes.

1258
00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:31.019
And then we have chunk fragments.

1259
00:54:31.019 --> 00:54:35.780
Chunk fragments aren't directly associated with an entity,

1260
00:54:35.780 --> 00:54:37.340
unlike most fragments.

1261
00:54:37.380 --> 00:54:40.219
They are associated with a chunk.

1262
00:54:40.219 --> 00:54:44.340
And what the archetype will do is group these chunks

1263
00:54:44.340 --> 00:54:47.380
together for separate processing.

1264
00:54:47.380 --> 00:54:48.980
So the kind of most obvious place

1265
00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:52.900
that this would be relevant is something like LODs.

1266
00:54:52.900 --> 00:54:57.900
So the archetype will kind of sub-organize

1267
00:55:00.539 --> 00:55:02.019
based on particular chunks.

1268
00:55:02.019 --> 00:55:06.139
And one of those chunk data might be the various LODs.

1269
00:55:06.139 --> 00:55:08.699
And so it's not associated with the entity itself,

1270
00:55:08.699 --> 00:55:11.340
it's associated with the chunk.

1271
00:55:11.340 --> 00:55:13.380
And therefore the archetype might have

1272
00:55:13.380 --> 00:55:15.460
a few different chunk fragments within it

1273
00:55:17.139 --> 00:55:19.099
that kind of define the different levels of detail

1274
00:55:19.099 --> 00:55:22.500
and these things get operated on in slightly different ways.

1275
00:55:22.500 --> 00:55:25.980
And again, this chunk fragment represents

1276
00:55:25.980 --> 00:55:27.900
part of an entity's composition.

1277
00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:32.780
So the entities that kind of do technically have

1278
00:55:32.780 --> 00:55:35.980
that chunk fragment associated with them

1279
00:55:36.659 --> 00:55:37.699
would have a different composition

1280
00:55:37.699 --> 00:55:41.130
and therefore would be grouped under a different archetype.

1281
00:55:42.329 --> 00:55:43.730
So that's the kind of everything

1282
00:55:43.730 --> 00:55:45.369
to talk about in mass density.

1283
00:55:45.369 --> 00:55:47.130
A lot of that is just mapping of terms

1284
00:55:47.130 --> 00:55:48.570
and these kind of special extra little bits

1285
00:55:48.570 --> 00:55:50.130
of data available to you.

1286
00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:52.809
There isn't actually an example

1287
00:55:52.809 --> 00:55:54.849
for mass density implementation,

1288
00:55:54.849 --> 00:55:57.210
but I will talk about something in a bit

1289
00:55:57.210 --> 00:55:58.250
that is kind of quite relevant

1290
00:55:58.250 --> 00:56:00.730
if you're interested in looking into this more.

1291
00:56:00.730 --> 00:56:03.409
But the last plugin to talk about is the zone graph.

1292
00:56:03.489 --> 00:56:07.889
So zone graph is, again, another plugin.

1293
00:56:07.889 --> 00:56:09.610
It won't be enabled by default.

1294
00:56:10.530 --> 00:56:14.289
So if you just go into your plugin section,

1295
00:56:14.289 --> 00:56:16.050
you can kind of look at that.

1296
00:56:16.050 --> 00:56:18.050
It is enabled in the example project

1297
00:56:18.050 --> 00:56:19.969
because there is an example of this one.

1298
00:56:19.969 --> 00:56:22.449
But essentially zone graph is

1299
00:56:22.449 --> 00:56:25.050
a kind of lightweight navigation system

1300
00:56:26.289 --> 00:56:30.769
for navigating around typically large spaces

1301
00:56:30.769 --> 00:56:33.889
that might feature streaming in and streaming out.

1302
00:56:33.889 --> 00:56:36.369
It allows pathfinding for portions of the world

1303
00:56:36.369 --> 00:56:38.329
that may even be not streamed into.

1304
00:56:39.489 --> 00:56:42.289
The really obvious examples is things like,

1305
00:56:42.289 --> 00:56:43.610
again, traffic systems.

1306
00:56:43.610 --> 00:56:45.210
I was already talking about traffic systems

1307
00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:47.090
with the mass entity system,

1308
00:56:47.090 --> 00:56:52.090
but traffic systems or pavements or trade networks

1309
00:56:52.489 --> 00:56:54.889
if you're doing something like a civilization game.

1310
00:56:54.889 --> 00:56:56.730
A lot of these things don't necessarily,

1311
00:56:56.730 --> 00:56:58.769
they have a concept of navigation,

1312
00:56:58.769 --> 00:57:01.849
but it's a lot more strict and rules bound.

1313
00:57:01.849 --> 00:57:06.170
It doesn't need the kind of free flowing navigation

1314
00:57:06.170 --> 00:57:08.210
of NavMesh, which can be a lot more expensive

1315
00:57:08.210 --> 00:57:10.010
because what NavMesh says is,

1316
00:57:10.010 --> 00:57:11.730
when we pick two points,

1317
00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:15.650
we define these large sections of space,

1318
00:57:15.650 --> 00:57:17.929
which can be freely negotiated around

1319
00:57:17.929 --> 00:57:20.050
and may at any point have obstructions.

1320
00:57:20.050 --> 00:57:21.809
And so you would need to negotiate around them

1321
00:57:21.809 --> 00:57:22.929
or areas of high cost.

1322
00:57:22.929 --> 00:57:25.489
And so we need to negotiate around those.

1323
00:57:25.489 --> 00:57:27.409
Instead, what the zone graph defines

1324
00:57:27.409 --> 00:57:29.650
is more explicit points.

1325
00:57:29.650 --> 00:57:30.809
We can kind of say,

1326
00:57:30.809 --> 00:57:32.610
oh, I transitioned from A to B,

1327
00:57:32.610 --> 00:57:36.730
and I know that I can only follow these particular lines.

1328
00:57:36.730 --> 00:57:38.409
And these lines might be lanes,

1329
00:57:38.409 --> 00:57:41.489
they may merge, they may branch in certain places,

1330
00:57:41.489 --> 00:57:45.210
but it's a lot more kind of point to point rules bound.

1331
00:57:45.210 --> 00:57:47.369
So the kind of navigation system

1332
00:57:47.369 --> 00:57:49.289
can be a lot more efficient.

1333
00:57:49.289 --> 00:57:51.250
It can be a lot more long distance.

1334
00:57:51.250 --> 00:57:55.250
You know, NavMesh, a consistent NavMesh

1335
00:57:56.090 --> 00:58:00.809
across like a 10 kilometer by 10 kilometer space

1336
00:58:00.809 --> 00:58:01.929
is going to be, you know,

1337
00:58:01.929 --> 00:58:03.929
a lot more costly to work out

1338
00:58:03.929 --> 00:58:05.650
how to get from one end of the island

1339
00:58:05.650 --> 00:58:07.960
to the other end of the island.

1340
00:58:07.960 --> 00:58:09.800
And for something like, you know,

1341
00:58:09.800 --> 00:58:13.199
a car where that car is only gonna stay to a road,

1342
00:58:13.199 --> 00:58:15.639
and there are only two roads on the whole island,

1343
00:58:15.639 --> 00:58:18.719
you can instantly see how this can afford to be

1344
00:58:18.719 --> 00:58:20.079
a lot more efficient.

1345
00:58:20.079 --> 00:58:23.400
And it can also be aware even of sections of that road

1346
00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:25.320
that don't need to be streamed in at the moment,

1347
00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:27.760
the zone graph system can still be aware

1348
00:58:27.760 --> 00:58:31.610
that those roads do exist, even if they are streamed out.

1349
00:58:31.610 --> 00:58:35.690
So the couple of bits of data

1350
00:58:35.690 --> 00:58:38.010
that are kind of relevant to zone graph to be aware of

1351
00:58:38.010 --> 00:58:40.409
is the zone shape data,

1352
00:58:40.409 --> 00:58:43.570
which essentially defines kind of our lanes,

1353
00:58:43.570 --> 00:58:44.889
our areas that are,

1354
00:58:47.969 --> 00:58:49.809
this is a little bit like our

1355
00:58:49.809 --> 00:58:55.250
NavMesh bounds volume, sorry,

1356
00:58:55.250 --> 00:58:57.210
reaching for the term there.

1357
00:58:57.210 --> 00:58:59.489
So this essentially defines our lanes.

1358
00:58:59.489 --> 00:59:01.929
We can set it up with a lane profile

1359
00:59:01.929 --> 00:59:04.170
to determine the number of lanes,

1360
00:59:04.170 --> 00:59:06.690
so any tags about it, what direction they go in,

1361
00:59:06.690 --> 00:59:08.489
why these lanes are.

1362
00:59:08.489 --> 00:59:10.570
A lot of this talks about traffic systems,

1363
00:59:10.570 --> 00:59:12.329
but you can obviously think about the same things

1364
00:59:12.329 --> 00:59:14.250
like paths through a wood,

1365
00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:16.010
if it's for, I don't know,

1366
00:59:16.010 --> 00:59:18.849
random adventurers in your RTS game,

1367
00:59:18.849 --> 00:59:23.849
or the same thing would apply for pavements or sidewalks,

1368
00:59:24.690 --> 00:59:26.849
depending on if you're American or English,

1369
00:59:27.969 --> 00:59:30.329
and the same kind of rules would apply.

1370
00:59:31.250 --> 00:59:32.690
Then we have our zone graph data,

1371
00:59:32.690 --> 00:59:35.010
which is roughly equivalent to our NavMesh data.

1372
00:59:35.010 --> 00:59:36.889
This is the kind of runtime thing

1373
00:59:36.889 --> 00:59:41.010
that is actually built out of our zone shapes,

1374
00:59:42.840 --> 00:59:45.360
and is the thing that is gonna be

1375
00:59:45.360 --> 00:59:47.039
our kind of centralized point of data

1376
00:59:47.039 --> 00:59:50.599
that is actually our total built up idea

1377
00:59:50.599 --> 00:59:53.119
of what is all the navigable space

1378
00:59:53.119 --> 00:59:54.599
based of our zone shapes.

1379
00:59:55.920 --> 00:59:59.599
And then we have our zone graph testing actor.

1380
00:59:59.599 --> 01:00:02.679
So this is something, this is a little bit like the,

1381
01:00:03.599 --> 01:00:06.119
there is an equivalent in NavMesh,

1382
01:00:06.119 --> 01:00:08.079
I think it's called the NavMesh testing actor,

1383
01:00:08.079 --> 01:00:10.760
which essentially visualizes a way

1384
01:00:10.760 --> 01:00:13.639
that we can get from one bit of our NavMesh to another,

1385
01:00:13.639 --> 01:00:14.880
what that would actually look like.

1386
01:00:14.920 --> 01:00:17.119
Zone graph testing actor does the same thing.

1387
01:00:17.119 --> 01:00:22.119
And then our zone graph annotation testing actor,

1388
01:00:22.440 --> 01:00:25.360
which essentially allows us to add a bunch of tests

1389
01:00:25.360 --> 01:00:29.360
about how we might be transitioning

1390
01:00:29.360 --> 01:00:30.639
between these different zones

1391
01:00:30.639 --> 01:00:32.760
and kind of adds in additional rules.

1392
01:00:34.199 --> 01:00:37.639
So just quickly looking at lane profiles, for example,

1393
01:00:37.639 --> 01:00:41.840
I've actually got, like I said, a little setup here.

1394
01:00:41.840 --> 01:00:44.400
So if we go to our zone graph example,

1395
01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:46.760
this again, another implementation

1396
01:00:46.760 --> 01:00:49.079
of the zone graph quick start,

1397
01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:53.960
but you can see we've defined our various zone graphs

1398
01:00:53.960 --> 01:00:59.469
here, we, and in this I've got,

1399
01:00:59.670 --> 01:01:04.800
sorry, if I actually just choose my zone graph here,

1400
01:01:05.280 --> 01:01:07.440
you can see we've essentially defined

1401
01:01:07.440 --> 01:01:10.039
these kind of, these curved shapes.

1402
01:01:10.039 --> 01:01:12.320
And we can see we've got these two different lanes

1403
01:01:12.320 --> 01:01:14.000
that are going in different directions.

1404
01:01:14.000 --> 01:01:15.880
That is reliant on,

1405
01:01:15.880 --> 01:01:18.639
and you can say we've got a lane profile selected here,

1406
01:01:18.639 --> 01:01:22.239
which is, I've defined this space.

1407
01:01:22.239 --> 01:01:25.719
How should we actually lay out the lanes within this space?

1408
01:01:25.719 --> 01:01:27.719
In this sense, it's a two-way traffic,

1409
01:01:27.719 --> 01:01:29.760
which I think is the Unreal default.

1410
01:01:29.760 --> 01:01:31.320
We can add in some debugging data,

1411
01:01:31.320 --> 01:01:33.599
which is like, what kind of shape should it be?

1412
01:01:33.599 --> 01:01:35.599
What colors should it represent in?

1413
01:01:35.599 --> 01:01:38.320
If we go to our edit and plugins,

1414
01:01:38.320 --> 01:01:41.159
sorry, edit project settings,

1415
01:01:41.159 --> 01:01:46.420
you can see we can define additional lane profiles.

1416
01:01:46.659 --> 01:01:53.789
Sorry, if I go to zone graph,

1417
01:02:04.420 --> 01:02:06.659
I now suddenly can't find my zone graph.

1418
01:02:07.900 --> 01:02:12.639
Engine zone graph, here we go.

1419
01:02:12.639 --> 01:02:15.199
Yeah, so we've got our list of lane profiles here.

1420
01:02:15.199 --> 01:02:16.719
So the default that I think zone graph

1421
01:02:16.719 --> 01:02:18.800
always comes in with is two-way traffic,

1422
01:02:18.800 --> 01:02:21.880
but obviously if you need something more like a,

1423
01:02:21.880 --> 01:02:23.400
you know, a four-lane motorway

1424
01:02:23.400 --> 01:02:25.559
or something that is only one directional

1425
01:02:25.559 --> 01:02:26.400
or something like that,

1426
01:02:26.400 --> 01:02:28.639
you can define additional lane profiles here.

1427
01:02:28.639 --> 01:02:31.000
And then you can choose those lane profiles

1428
01:02:31.000 --> 01:02:33.519
for your different zone shapes.

1429
01:02:33.519 --> 01:02:35.639
This just has an example of like what,

1430
01:02:35.639 --> 01:02:37.840
you know, a random might look like.

1431
01:02:37.840 --> 01:02:40.079
We've got things for like a crossing.

1432
01:02:40.079 --> 01:02:42.880
So again, this is particularly more applicable

1433
01:02:42.880 --> 01:02:45.280
for something like a traffic system where, you know,

1434
01:02:45.280 --> 01:02:46.519
you're only allowed to transition

1435
01:02:46.519 --> 01:02:48.480
from one lane to another lane.

1436
01:02:48.480 --> 01:02:50.960
And when you have different shapes,

1437
01:02:50.960 --> 01:02:52.280
how would you actually transition

1438
01:02:52.280 --> 01:02:54.599
